<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Blida</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Blida is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> at the foot of the Tell <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a>, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, on the edge of the Mitidja plain. Founded in the 16th century, it was occupied by the French during the conquest of Algeria in the 1830s and grew into a garrison and market town. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frantz-fanon">Frantz Fanon</a> worked at its psychiatric hospital in the 1950s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/blida.jpg" alt="Blida" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BullyanDZ · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chrea_blida_snow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Blida lies 190 metres above sea level at the base of the Tell Atlas, on the southern edge of the fertile Mitidja Plain and the right bank of the Oued el Kebir, which flows out of the Chiffa gorge. The stream's abundant water powered large corn mills and several factories and fed the town's fountains and irrigated gardens. Orchards and gardens surround the town, which is known as the city of roses. Its name is a diminutive of the Arabic belda, city. Chréa National Park, one of the largest in the country, lies within Blida, and the nearby Chiffa gorge is one of the few habitats of the endangered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> macaque.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>No ancient centre preceded the city. It was identified with a medieval town of Mitidja, which was ruined during the campaigns of the Beni Ghania. The present town was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a>, principally Sidi Ahmed El-Kebir, in the 16th century. A mosque was built by order of Khair-ed-din Barbarossa, and under the Turks the town was of some importance. Its gates no longer exist, but their names, such as Bab Er-Rahba and Bab Es-Sebt, are still used as reference points for streets and businesses.</p><p>After an earthquake in March 1825 the town was rebuilt on a grid plan, on a site about a mile from the ruins. In 1839 French troops occupied the city and built a military base, an airbase, a communications centre and a military hospital. Life was racially segregated: Muslims were confined to a crowded ghetto, and boarding schools kept separate dormitories. The occupation lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> won independence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algerian-war">Algerian War</a>. Another earthquake damaged the town in 1867.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Blida is the capital of Blida Province, about 45 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> of the province, the Djebailia, speak Taqbaylit, a Berber language close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabyle">Kabyle</a> varieties spoken east of Algiers. The Stade Mustapha Tchaker, opened in 2002, is the home ground of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> national football team.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Blida” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The war of independence became an inspiration for anti-colonial movements in Africa and beyond; Frantz Fanon, who worked as a psychiatrist at <b>Blida</b>, theorised it in The Wretched of the Earth (1961), and Gillo Pontecorvo’s film The Battle of Algiers (1966) made it known worldwide.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
